*==============================================================================
* OPAMP_FULL  -  behavioural op-amp macromodel (PSpice port)
*
* Copyright (c) 2026 Stanislav Maslovski
* SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause  (see LICENSE)
* Version 1.7 (2026-11). Developed with the help of Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic).
* PSpice port, generated by tools/make_pspice_port.py from ../opamp_full.lib.
* Do not edit by hand: change the main library and run the script again.
* Same pins, parameters and behaviour as the main library (checked against
* ngspice with tests/cross_sim.py). Tested with PSpice 9.1 (1999 evaluation
* version), so it should also run in later PSpice releases. Differences:
*   - B sources and parameter-gain E/G/H sources are E/G VALUE={} sources;
*     ?: is IF().
*   - Like the main library, it computes shared sub-expressions once on their
*     own nodes (faster), except nim, which is written back into Ggm. If a
*     circuit exceeds the 64 nodes of the PSpice evaluation version,
*     regenerate the port with --inline all.
*   - VCMN defaults to 1e30, which means mid-supply (PSpice 9 cannot default a
*     parameter to an expression of other parameters).
*   - PSpice differentiates VALUE expressions numerically. To keep the small-
*     signal results exact, the output-stage command current is carried in mA
*     (nicmd...), the crossover input in mV (nvx...), and the clamp excesses on
*     their own nodes (nex...). 1e15 ohm partners give the 1k noise references
*     the two connections PSpice requires.
*
* Pins: inp inn vcc vee out
*
* LINEAR EFFECTS
*   - finite DC gain A0, dominant pole, GBW, two extra poles (FP2, FP3)
*   - differential / common-mode input R and C (noiseless)
*   - CMRR, PSRR, PSRR+ and PSRR- with frequency roll-off, consistent with a
*     part that has no ground pin (see "Supply and common-mode rejection")
*   - output resistance (ROUT), optional DC output resistance falling above
*     the dominant pole (ROUTDC), and output capacitance
*   - input voltage noise (white + 1/f) and input current noise (white + 1/f,
*     FCIN = current-noise 1/f corner, independent on each input; 0 = off)
* NONLINEAR EFFECTS
*   - input offset voltage, input bias and offset currents
*   - input stage tanh() characteristic  -> slew rate and large-signal THD
*   - optional separate rising / falling slew rates (SRR, SRF)
*   - optional follower jump from the input-pair tail capacitance (KJUMP)
*   - optional phase reversal through an input junction (PRHI, PRLO, VPRHI,
*     VPRLO, IPR): one-sided, with the input current and the source-resistance
*     dependence of real parts
*   - optional rail-to-rail input crossover: offset and bias-current step
*     (VCMT, VKT, DVOST, DIBT)
*   - input common-mode range (gm collapses near the rails)
*   - optional differential input clamp: anti-parallel diodes between inp
*     and inn (VCLMP, RCLMP); no clamps to the rails
*   - output voltage swing limited by the supplies (VHD/VLD headroom)
*   - output current limit (ISRC/ISNK), class-AB crossover dead-zone (VXO)
*   - supply current = quiescent + load current drawn from the right rail
*
* Defaults approximate a general-purpose 1 MHz bipolar op amp on +/-15 V
* (uA741-like typicals). Rail-to-rail example parameters are given at the end.
*
* Units: SR in V/s (0.5Meg = 0.5 V/us). PSRR/CMRR as ratios (31.6k = 90 dB).
*
* Notes
*   - No ground node: no element connects to node 0. Pin currents return to
*     vcc or vee, and internal voltages are referred to the pins (the gain
*     stages to nref = vee + KREF*(vcc-vee)). Results do not depend on which
*     node of the circuit is grounded; floating supplies work.
*   - Supply and common-mode rejection. Without a ground pin, the errors
*     referred to the input per volt of vcc (T_P), of vee (T_N) and of the
*     input common mode (T_C) obey T_P + T_N + T_C = 1/A(s) at every
*     frequency (1/A = 1/A0 + s/(2*pi*GBW) below FP2). PSRR (both supplies
*     moved apart, per volt of each) is 1/(T_P - T_N). So only two of CMRR,
*     PSRR, PSRRP, PSRRN are independent, at DC and again for the slopes above
*     the corners. Rules (each ratio 0 = not given):
*       - DC values are signed: the DC error is 1/value. A positive error
*         moves the output like a positive VOS when its pin (or the input
*         common mode) rises. These errors come mostly from device mismatch,
*         so their signs differ from unit to unit (see "Signed parameters").
*       - the first two given in the order PSRRP, PSRRN, CMRR, PSRR are used
*         exactly, signs included; the others are derived. The defaults
*         CMRR = -31.6k and PSRR = 31.6k count as given; if fewer than two
*         are given (CMRR or PSRR set to 0), they are added again.
*       - a slope is given when its corner is set (FCMRR; FPSRR for PSRR;
*         FPSRRP or FPSRRN, else FPSRR, for the supplies). Fewer than two given
*         slopes: the supplies get only the coupling through the gain stage
*         (KREF/A for vcc, (1-KREF)/A for vee).
*       - slope signs are not taken from the parameters: the coupling above
*         the corners goes through the compensation and is systematic. The
*         supply slopes are positive; an exact CMRR or PSRR slope takes the
*         sign that keeps the derived supply slopes positive (if both do,
*         the smaller derived slopes; for CMRR with PSRR, the PSRR slope is
*         positive for KREF >= 0.5, negative below).
*     A given value that the rules cannot honour is a conflict: CMRR is
*     derived from PSRRP and PSRRN when all three are given.
*     python3 tools/rejection.py CMRR=... PSRRP=... prints what a parameter set
*     gives (the same rules, in Python).
*   - VOS, the input voltage noise and the CMRR/PSRR error terms form one
*     error voltage in series with the + input, ahead of RID (RID is driven
*     by the internal V(vd)). So no spurious (VOS+en)/RID current flows in
*     the source resistance.
*   - All model-internal resistors are noiseless (VCCS form), except the
*     1e15 ohm leak resistors (negligible noise). The only noise
*     generators are Rnv, Rnp, Rnn (white), Dfa (1/f voltage) and Dfp1, Dfn1
*     (1/f current, FCIN > 0). Noise levels are
*     exact at TEMP = 27 C and scale like kT elsewhere.
*   - Portability: tested in ngspice-42 and LTspice 26, and the PSpice port
*     in PSpice 9.1 (tests/cross_sim.py; DC/AC/transient/noise agree). White
*     e_n is passed through the E source Ewn (LTspice ignores the noise of a
*     resistor that is read only inside a B-source expression). Nodes that
*     otherwise see only G elements and capacitors get a 1e15 ohm DC path
*     (R*lk, to nref or, inside the model only, to node 0), which satisfies
*     the floating-node checks of LTspice and PSpice; their effect on gain
*     and noise is negligible.
*     This file is the PSpice port.
*   - Bias currents: IB > 0 flows INTO the pins (NPN input); use IB < 0 for PNP.
*   - RIC || CIC is the common-mode input impedance of EACH input pin (per-pin
*     datasheet values, e.g. "34 Mohm, 4 pF each input"): RIC to nref (its
*     current returns to vee), CIC half to each rail. With both inputs tied
*     together the impedance is RIC/2 || 2*CIC.
*   - VCMN: the input common-mode voltage at which VOS is specified (with the
*     supplies at VCCN, VEEN). The default is mid-supply.
*   - Differential clamp: two anti-parallel diodes between inp and inn, each
*     with forward voltage VCLMP at 1 mA and
*     series resistance RCLMP. The default VCLMP=100 disables it (the 741 has
*     no clamp diodes; leakage < 1e-14 A at 15 V). For an OP27-style clamp use
*     VCLMP=0.7 RCLMP=100 (the original model's DIN diodes).
*   - Crossover: for |Iout| << VXO/ROUT the small-signal output resistance is
*     2*ROUT. It is ROUT once |Iout| > ~2*VXO/ROUT (2 mV/75 ohm ~ 50 uA).
*     Set VXO=0 for a plain ROUT everywhere.
*
* PARAMETERS  (name, unit, default, meaning)
*  Gain and frequency response
*   A0      V/V    200k     open-loop DC gain without load (with ROUTDC = 0
*                           also the loaded gain, to within ROUT/RL)
*   GBW     Hz     1Meg     gain-bandwidth product (unity-gain frequency of the
*                           dominant-pole response)
*   FP2     Hz     0        second pole; 0 = 3*GBW (3Meg at the default
*                           GBW)
*   FP3     Hz     0        third pole; 0 = 10*GBW (10Meg)
*   SR      V/s    0.5Meg   slew rate (0.5Meg = 0.5 V/us)
*   SRR     V/s    0        slew rate of the rising (positive-going) output;
*                           0 = SR
*   SRF     V/s    0        slew rate of the falling (negative-going) output;
*                           0 = SR. LF155 datasheet pulse photo: SRR ~5.5Meg,
*                           SRF ~11Meg
*   KJUMP   -      0        input-pair tail capacitance: output jump / fast
*                           input common-mode step (large-signal follower
*                           response); 0 = off. The sign gives the input-pair
*                           polarity: > 0 npn/nmos (jump on rising edges),
*                           < 0 pnp/pmos/p-JFET (jump on falling edges).
*                           uA741 (datasheet pulse response): KJUMP ~0.27
*                           with the default SR
*  Input DC errors and impedance
*   VOS     V      1m       input offset voltage, in series with the + input
*   IB      A      80n      input bias current (average of the two pins);
*                           IB > 0 flows into the pins (NPN), IB < 0 out (PNP)
*   IOS     A      20n      input offset current, I(inp) - I(inn)
*   RID     ohm    2Meg     differential input resistance (noiseless); its
*                           current saturates at |IB|, as in a bipolar pair
*   CID     F      1.4p     differential input capacitance
*   RIC     ohm    400Meg   common-mode input resistance of each input pin
*                           (to nref; noiseless)
*   CIC     F      0.75p    common-mode input capacitance of each input pin
*                           (half to each rail)
*  Differential input clamp (two anti-parallel diodes between inp and inn;
*  there are no clamp diodes from the inputs to the supply rails)
*   VCLMP   V      100      forward voltage of each diode at 1 mA (set through
*                           the emission coefficient); 100 = no clamp.
*                           OP27-style clamp: VCLMP=0.7 RCLMP=100
*   RCLMP   ohm    100      series resistance of each clamp diode
*  Common-mode and supply rejection (signed ratios, 0 = not given; see the
*  rules in Notes and "Signed parameters")
*   CMRR    ratio  -31.6k   DC CMRR (31.6k = 90 dB)
*   PSRR    ratio  31.6k    DC PSRR with both supplies moved apart (per volt of
*                           each supply), the usual single datasheet value
*   PSRRP   ratio  0        DC rejection of the positive supply alone
*   PSRRN   ratio  0        DC rejection of the negative supply alone
*                           The defaults give PSRR+ 89 dB, PSRR- 112 dB at
*                           A0 = 200k
*   FCMRR   Hz     0        CMRR falls at 20 dB/dec above this frequency.
*                           Keep CMRR >= 10 dB at GBW (|CMRR|*FCMRR/GBW >= 3):
*                           the CM error is in series with the input, and
*                           below that a follower's step response rings
*   FPSRR   Hz     0        corner of PSRR, and of PSRR+/PSRR- if FPSRRP/FPSRRN
*                           are 0
*   FPSRRP  Hz     0        PSRR+ corner (positive supply)
*   FPSRRN  Hz     0        PSRR- corner (negative supply)
*   KREF    -      0.5      reference of the gain stages, nref = vee +
*                           KREF*(vcc-vee); the supply at which the
*                           compensation is referred couples as 1/A(s)
*                           (in the uA741 it is vee: KREF = 0). Totals are
*                           as specified whatever KREF is; it matters above
*                           FP2 and for the derived quantities. It is set by
*                           the architecture: KREF = 0 when the compensated
*                           gain stage (common-emitter or common-source
*                           driver) works from vee, 1 when from vcc
*   VCCN    V      15       nominal positive supply (PSRR reference)
*   VEEN    V      -15      nominal negative supply (PSRR reference)
*   VCMN    V      mid      input CM voltage at which VOS is specified
*  Noise
*   ENOISE  V/rtHz 20n      white input voltage noise density
*   FCEN    Hz     200      1/f corner of the voltage noise
*   INOISE  A/rtHz 0.5p     white input current noise density, each input
*   FCIN    Hz     0        1/f corner of the current noise (independent on
*                           the two inputs); 0 = white only
*  Supplies, input range and output swing
*   ICCQ    A      1.7m     quiescent supply current, vcc to vee
*   ICMHI   V      2        input CM range: distance below vcc at which the
*                           input-stage gm has fallen to half (< 0: beyond rail)
*   ICMLO   V      2        the same, above vee
*   VK      V      0.15     width of the gm roll-off at the input-range edges
*   PRHI    -      0        phase reversal at the top: a junction of the input
*                           pair (npn base-collector) conducts near vcc; its
*                           current reverses the output, which runs to the
*                           wrong rail at about PRHI*SR; 0 = no junction.
*                           Typical 1..4
*   PRLO    -      0        the same at the bottom (p-JFET gate-drain)
*   VPRHI   V      0        position of the top junction: input voltage below
*                           vcc where the output flips (< 0: above vcc)
*   VPRLO   V      0        the same above vee
*   IPR     A      0        input current of a conducting junction (into the
*                           pin at the top, out of it at the bottom); about the
*                           tail current of the input pair. 0 = SR*30pF.
*                           A source resistance RS prevents the reversal while
*                           0.9*RS*IPR is larger than the overdrive
*   VCMT    V      1        rail-to-rail input crossover: position of the
*                           transition between the input pairs, below vcc
*   VKT     V      0.1      width of the crossover transition
*   DVOST   V      0        offset change across the transition (VOS applies
*                           below it); 0 = none
*   DIBT    A      0        bias-current change across the transition, each pin
*   VHD     V      2        minimum headroom, output to vcc (swing limit)
*   VLD     V      2        minimum headroom, output to vee (swing limit)
*  Output stage
*   ROUT    ohm    75       output resistance (open loop, above the dominant
*                           pole: the datasheet's r_o)
*   ROUTDC  ohm    0        additional open-loop output resistance at DC: the
*                           high-impedance gain node seen through the output
*                           stage. Zout = ROUT + ROUTDC/(1 + j*f/fd), fd =
*                           GBW/A0. A0 is then the unloaded gain; into RL the
*                           DC gain is A0*RL/(RL + ROUT + ROUTDC). Typically
*                           tens of times ROUT for a simple bipolar output,
*                           hundreds for a common-source (rail-to-rail) one
*   COUT    F      3p       output capacitance (half to each rail)
*   ISRC    A      25m      maximum source current
*   ISNK    A      25m      maximum sink current
*   VXO     V      2m       class-AB crossover dead zone: for |Iout| below about
*                           VXO/ROUT the small-signal output resistance is
*                           2*ROUT; 0 disables it
*
* SIGNED PARAMETERS
*   These DC parameters take a sign, and in a real part it is set by device
*   mismatch (it differs from unit to unit, and can change with temperature):
*     VOS, IOS, DVOST, DIBT, CMRR, PSRR, PSRRP, PSRRN
*   (IB and KJUMP also take a sign, but theirs is set by the input-pair type.)
*   A datasheet gives magnitudes: enter them as positive, as for VOS, or keep
*   the defaults. To model the spread, change the signs:
*     - PSRRP and PSRRN given: equal signs give the worst derived CMRR
*       (|1/CMRR| ~ 1/|PSRRP| + 1/|PSRRN|), opposite signs the best.
*     - CMRR and PSRR given: CMRR and PSRR of equal sign put the larger
*       supply error on vee (PSRR- the worse), opposite signs on vcc.
*     - offset over a range: a CMRR of the same sign as VOS makes the
*       offset grow as the common mode rises (a PSRR of the same sign: as the
*       supplies move apart); the opposite sign makes it shrink there.
*   Slopes above the corners are systematic and get their signs from the
*   rules (Notes); the signs of the DC values do not affect them.
*==============================================================================

*------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* ZCPL : out = K0*in + K1*d(in)/dt     (a zero of either sign, exact derivative)
* Used for CMRR(f) and PSRR(f): the coupling into the input rises above
* |K0/K1|/(2*pi), i.e. the rejection ratio falls at 20 dB/dec. FHI limits the
* derivative. The derivative resistor is a noiseless VCCS. All nodes are
* internal (computation only).
*------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.subckt ZCPL in out PARAMS: K0=1u K1=0 FHI=20Meg
.param CD=1n
Ek na 0 VALUE={(K0)*V(in)}
Cd  in nx {CD}
Gd nx nv VALUE={(6.283185307*FHI*CD)*V(nx,nv)}
Vs  nv 0 0
Rlk nx 0 1e15
Ed out na VALUE={(K1/CD)*I(Vs)}
.ends ZCPL

*------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.subckt OPAMP_FULL inp inn vcc vee out
+ PARAMS:
+ A0=200k GBW=1Meg SR=0.5Meg SRR=0 SRF=0 FP2=0 FP3=0
+ VOS=1m IB=80n IOS=20n
+ RID=2Meg CID=1.4p RIC=400Meg CIC=0.75p
+ CMRR=-31.6k PSRR=31.6k PSRRP=0 PSRRN=0 FCMRR=0 FPSRR=0 FPSRRP=0 FPSRRN=0 VCCN=15 VEEN=-15 KREF=0.5
+ VCMN=1e30
+ ENOISE=20n FCEN=200 INOISE=0.5p FCIN=0
+ ICCQ=1.7m VHD=2 VLD=2 ICMHI=2 ICMLO=2 VK=0.15
+ ROUT=75 ROUTDC=0 ISRC=25m ISNK=25m VXO=2m COUT=3p
+ VCLMP=100 RCLMP=100 KJUMP=0
+ PRHI=0 PRLO=0 VPRHI=0 VPRLO=0 IPR=0 VCMT=1 VKT=0.1 DVOST=0 DIBT=0

* ---- derived constants -------------------------------------------------------
.param PI2=6.283185307
.param VCMX={IF(VCMN>1e29, 0.5*(VCCN+VEEN), VCMN)}   ; VCMN=1e30: mid-supply
.param C1V=30p
.param SRRV={IF(SRR>0, SRR, SR)}
.param SRFV={IF(SRF>0, SRF, SR)}
.param IMAXV={0.5*(SRRV+SRFV)*C1V}  ; mean input-stage current limit -> slew rate
.param IDIFV={0.5*(SRRV-SRFV)*C1V}  ; half the difference (asymmetric slew)
.param GM0={PI2*GBW*C1V}           ; small-signal transconductance -> GBW
.param R1V={A0/GM0}                ; DC gain A0 = GM0*R1V
.param FP2V={IF(FP2>0, FP2, 3*GBW)}
.param FP3V={IF(FP3>0, FP3, 10*GBW)}
.param VNTH={sqrt(4*1.380649e-23*300.15*1e3)}   ; thermal noise of 1k at 27C
* 1/f source: diode biased at IFL. Its flicker voltage density is
* KF*IFL*(Vt/IFL)^2/f = KF*Vt^2/(IFL*f), so KF = ENOISE^2*FCEN*IFL/Vt^2.
* The two diodes also add white shot noise 2*(2*q*Vt^2/IFL), i.e.
* (0.21 nV/rtHz)^2 at 10 mA. It is subtracted from the white generator so
* that the total white density is exactly ENOISE (for ENOISE > 0.21 nV).
* (IFL much above 0.1 A upsets the transient time-step control.)
.param IFL=10m
.param VTN={1.380649e-23*300.15/1.602176634e-19}
.param KFV={ENOISE*ENOISE*FCEN*IFL/(VTN*VTN)}
.param ESH2={4*1.602176634e-19*VTN*VTN/IFL}
.param GENV={sqrt(max(ENOISE*ENOISE-ESH2,0))/VNTH}
.param GNI={INOISE/VNTH}
* 1/f current noise (FCIN > 0): one more flicker diode pair per input, so the
* two pins get independent noise. The flicker voltage of the pair is scaled to
* VNI*sqrt(FCIN/f) with VNI = 1 uV/rtHz and converted to current by GFI =
* INOISE/VNI, giving INOISE^2*FCIN/f. The pair's shot noise then adds only
* (0.21n/1u)^2 = 4e-8 of INOISE^2. FCIN = 0 disables the sources (GFI = 0).
.param KFI={1e-12*FCIN*IFL/(VTN*VTN)}
.param GFI={IF(FCIN>0, INOISE/1u, 0)}

* ---- input network (noiseless) -------------------------------------------------
* RID carries V(vd)/RID, i.e. it sits behind the series error voltage
* (VOS, noise, CMRR/PSRR), as if that voltage were in series with the + pin.
* The current through RID saturates at |IB| (IB = 0: no limit), as the base
* currents of a bipolar pair do once one transistor takes the whole tail
* current; small-signal it is V(vd)/RID.
.param IDL={IF(abs(IB)>0, abs(IB), 1)}
.param KID={1/(RID*IDL)}
Gid inp inn VALUE={IDL*tanh(V(vd)*KID)}
Cid  inp inn {CID}
* No element of the model connects to ground: every pin current returns to a
* supply pin, and internal voltages are referred to nref (between the rails)
* or are computed from pin-voltage differences. Grounding any node of the
* circuit then gives the same result (a floating supply works).
* nref: reference of the gain stages, KREF of the way from vee to vcc
Eref nref 0 VALUE={V(vee)+KREF*(V(vcc)-V(vee))}
* common-mode input resistance (to nref, current returned to vee) and
* capacitance (half to each rail)
Gic1 inp vee VALUE={(1/RIC)*V(inp,nref)}
Gic2 inn vee VALUE={(1/RIC)*V(inn,nref)}
Cic1 inp vcc {0.5*CIC}
Cic3 inp vee {0.5*CIC}
Cic2 inn vcc {0.5*CIC}
Cic4 inn vee {0.5*CIC}
* bias currents flow INTO the pins for IB > 0 (NPN input), returned to vee
Ibp  inp vee DC {IB+IOS/2}
Ibn  inn vee DC {IB-IOS/2}
* shared nodes, each computed once per iteration (fewer, smaller expressions:
* every controlling node of a behavioural source costs a derivative tree)
Evc nvc 0 VALUE={0.5*(V(inp)+V(inn))}
Esq nsq 0 VALUE={0.5*sqrt((V(inp)-V(inn))*(V(inp)-V(inn))+1e-4)}
Ecx ncx 0 VALUE={0.5*(1+tanh((V(nvc)-V(vcc)+VCMT)/VKT))}
Gibt1 inp vee VALUE={DIBT*V(ncx)}
Gibt2 inn vee VALUE={DIBT*V(ncx)}
* optional differential input clamp diodes: VCLMP = diode drop at 1 mA
* (set via the emission coefficient), RCLMP = series resistance
Dp1  inp inn DIN
Dp2  inn inp DIN
.model DIN D(IS=1e-15 N={VCLMP/(VTN*log(1e-3/1e-15))} RS={RCLMP})

* ---- noise sources (visible to .noise analysis only) ------------------------
* white voltage noise: thermal noise of a 1k resistor scaled by GENV in Ewn
Rnv  nnv 0 1e3
Rnv2 nnv 0 1e15   ; PSpice: second connection at nnv
Ewn nwn 0 VALUE={(GENV)*V(nnv)}
* white current noise on each input
Rnp  nnp 0 1e3
Rnp2 nnp 0 1e15   ; PSpice: second connection at nnp
Rnn  nnn 0 1e3
Rnn2 nnn 0 1e15   ; PSpice: second connection at nnn
Gnp vee inp VALUE={(GNI)*V(nnp)}
Gnn vee inn VALUE={(GNI)*V(nnn)}
* 1/f voltage noise: flicker noise of a diode biased at IFL, minus an
* identical KF=0 diode so that the DC drop cancels
Ifa  0 nfa DC {IFL}
Dfa  nfa 0 DFLK
Ifb  0 nfb DC {IFL}
Dfb  nfb 0 DQ
Efl  nfl 0 nfa nfb 1
.model DFLK D(IS=1e-14 N=1 KF={KFV} AF=1)
.model DQ   D(IS=1e-14 N=1)
* 1/f current noise, independent on each input (FCIN > 0)
Ifp1 0 nfp1 DC {IFL}
Dfp1 nfp1 0 DFLKI
Ifp2 0 nfp2 DC {IFL}
Dfp2 nfp2 0 DQ
Gfp vee inp VALUE={(GFI)*V(nfp1,nfp2)}
Ifn1 0 nfn1 DC {IFL}
Dfn1 nfn1 0 DFLKI
Ifn2 0 nfn2 DC {IFL}
Dfn2 nfn2 0 DQ
Gfn vee inn VALUE={(GFI)*V(nfn1,nfn2)}
.model DFLKI D(IS=1e-14 N=1 KF={KFI} AF=1)

* ---- CMRR and PSRR coupling into the input -----------------------------------
* Explicit coupling of the supply-to-input-CM differences, RJP(s) and RJN(s),
* from the rules below (see tools/rejection.py and the PARAMETERS section).
* Together with the coupling through nref (KREF/A(s) for vcc, (1-KREF)/A(s)
* for vee) it gives the totals T_P, T_N, and T_C = 1/A - T_P - T_N for the
* input common mode: errors are referred to the pin voltages only.
* ---- begin rejection rules (generated by tools/rejection.py; do not edit) ----
.param RJ2PI={6.283185307}
.param RJG0={1/A0}
.param RJG1={1/(RJ2PI*GBW)}
.param RJHP={IF(abs(PSRRP)>0, 1, 0)}
.param RJHN={IF(abs(PSRRN)>0, 1, 0)}
.param RJHC={IF(abs(CMRR)>0, 1, 0)}
.param RJHS={IF(abs(PSRR)>0, 1, 0)}
.param RJVP0={RJHP/(PSRRP+1-RJHP)}
.param RJVN0={RJHN/(PSRRN+1-RJHN)}
.param RJVC0={RJHC/(CMRR+1-RJHC)+(1-RJHC)*(-1/31.6k)}
.param RJVS0={RJHS/(PSRR+1-RJHS)+(1-RJHS)*(1/31.6k)}
.param RJGC0={IF(RJHP+RJHN+RJHC+RJHS<2, 1, RJHC)}
.param RJGS0={IF(RJHP+RJHN+RJGC0+RJHS<2, 1, RJHS)}
.param RJXP0={RJHP}
.param RJXN0={IF(RJHP>0, 0, RJHN)}
.param RJXC0={IF(RJHP+RJHN>0, 0, RJGC0)}
.param RJYN0={IF(RJHP>0, RJHN, 0)}
.param RJYC0={RJGC0*(min(RJHP+RJHN,1)-min(max(RJHP+RJHN-1,0),1))}
.param RJYS0={RJGS0*(min(RJHP+RJHN+RJGC0,1)-min(max(RJHP+RJHN+RJGC0-1,0),1))}
.param RJAX0={RJXP0+RJXC0}
.param RJBX0={RJXN0+RJXC0}
.param RJRX0={RJXP0*RJVP0+RJXN0*RJVN0+RJXC0*(RJG0-RJVC0)}
.param RJAY0={RJYC0+RJYS0}
.param RJBY0={RJYN0+RJYC0-RJYS0}
.param RJRY0={RJYN0*RJVN0+RJYC0*(RJG0-RJVC0)+RJYS0*RJVS0}
.param RJD0={RJAX0*RJBY0-RJAY0*RJBX0}
.param TP0={(RJRX0*RJBY0-RJRY0*RJBX0)/RJD0}
.param TN0={(RJAX0*RJRY0-RJAY0*RJRX0)/RJD0}
.param TC0={RJG0-TP0-TN0}
.param TS0={TP0-TN0}
.param RJFPV={IF(FPSRRP>0, FPSRRP, FPSRR)}
.param RJFNV={IF(FPSRRN>0, FPSRRN, FPSRR)}
.param RJJP={IF(RJFPV>0, RJHP, 0)}
.param RJJN={IF(RJFNV>0, RJHN, 0)}
.param RJJC={IF(FCMRR>0, RJHC, 0)}
.param RJJS={IF(FPSRR>0, RJHS, 0)}
.param RJLA1={IF(RJJP+RJJN+RJJC+RJJS<2, 1-RJJP, 0)}
.param RJGP1={max(RJJP,RJLA1)}
.param RJGN1={IF(RJJP+RJJN+RJJC+RJJS+RJLA1<2, 1, RJJN)}
.param RJXP1={RJGP1}
.param RJXN1={IF(RJGP1>0, 0, RJGN1)}
.param RJXC1={IF(RJGP1+RJGN1>0, 0, RJJC)}
.param RJYN1={IF(RJGP1>0, RJGN1, 0)}
.param RJYC1={RJJC*(min(RJGP1+RJGN1,1)-min(max(RJGP1+RJGN1-1,0),1))}
.param RJYS1={RJJS*(min(RJGP1+RJGN1+RJJC,1)-min(max(RJGP1+RJGN1+RJJC-1,0),1))}
.param RJVP1={IF(RJJP>0, abs(RJVP0)/(RJ2PI*max(RJFPV,1e-30)), KREF*RJG1)}
.param RJVN1={IF(RJJN>0, abs(RJVN0)/(RJ2PI*max(RJFNV,1e-30)), (1-KREF)*RJG1)}
.param RJMC1={IF(RJJC>0, abs(RJVC0)/(RJ2PI*max(FCMRR,1e-30)), 0)}
.param RJMS1={IF(RJJS>0, abs(RJVS0)/(RJ2PI*max(FPSRR,1e-30)), 0)}
.param RJUC1={RJG1-RJXP1*RJVP1-RJXN1*RJVN1-RJYS1*RJMS1-RJMC1}
.param RJSC1={IF(RJUC1>=0, 1, -1)}
.param RJVC1={RJSC1*RJMC1}
.param RJSSP={IF(RJVP1>=RJMS1, 1, -1)}
.param RJSSN={IF(RJVN1>=RJMS1, -1, 1)}
.param RJSSK={IF(KREF>=0.5, 1, -1)}
.param RJVS1={RJMS1*(RJXP1*RJSSP+RJXN1*RJSSN+RJXC1*RJSSK)}
.param RJAX1={RJXP1+RJXC1}
.param RJBX1={RJXN1+RJXC1}
.param RJRX1={RJXP1*RJVP1+RJXN1*RJVN1+RJXC1*(RJG1-RJVC1)}
.param RJAY1={RJYC1+RJYS1}
.param RJBY1={RJYN1+RJYC1-RJYS1}
.param RJRY1={RJYN1*RJVN1+RJYC1*(RJG1-RJVC1)+RJYS1*RJVS1}
.param RJD1={RJAX1*RJBY1-RJAY1*RJBX1}
.param TP1={(RJRX1*RJBY1-RJRY1*RJBX1)/RJD1}
.param TN1={(RJAX1*RJRY1-RJAY1*RJRX1)/RJD1}
.param TC1={RJG1-TP1-TN1}
.param TS1={TP1-TN1}
.param RJP0={TP0-KREF*RJG0}
.param RJP1={TP1-KREF*RJG1}
.param RJN0={TN0-(1-KREF)*RJG0}
.param RJN1={TN1-(1-KREF)*RJG1}
* ---- end rejection rules ----
.param FHIV={max(20Meg,3*FP3V)}
* (a brace expression with a minus sign after V(...) - V(...) is misread by
* ngspice 42 inside a B source: the nominal differences are separate params)
.param DVPN={VCCN-VCMX}
.param DVNN={VEEN-VCMX}
Erp nrp0 0 VALUE={V(vcc)-V(nvc)-DVPN}
Xrp  nrp0 nrp ZCPL PARAMS: K0={RJP0} K1={RJP1} FHI={FHIV}
Ern nrn0 0 VALUE={V(vee)-V(nvc)-DVNN}
Xrn  nrn0 nrn ZCPL PARAMS: K0={RJN0} K1={RJN1} FHI={FHIV}

* ---- effective differential input voltage ------------------------------------
* = V(inp) - V(inn) + series error voltage (VOS + CMRR/PSRR terms + noise)
Evd vd 0 VALUE={V(inp)-V(inn)+VOS+V(nrp)+V(nrn)+V(nwn)+V(nfl)+DVOST*V(ncx)}

* ---- input common-mode range window (0..1, 0.5 at the ICMR edge) ---------------
* (times 1 - J for each conducting reversal junction, see below)
* Each edge uses the input that keeps the stage working: the top edge the lower
* input, the bottom edge the higher one (smooth min/max). A follower driven past
* the range then stops at the edge, as a transistor-level stage does.
.param EPH={IF(PRHI>0, 1, 0)}
.param EPL={IF(PRLO>0, 1, 0)}
.param KJ={0.5/VTN}            ; 1/(2*VT): logistic of thermal width
* Input current: the same junction step, shifted by VT*ln(9) so that it is
* 0.9*IPR where the output flips (J = 1/2) and IPR beyond.
.param IPRV={IF(IPR>0, IPR, IMAXV)}
.param DJ={VTN*log(9)}
* junction functions of the reversal (0 when unused)
Ejhp njhp 0 VALUE={EPH*0.5*(1+tanh((V(inp)-V(vcc)+VPRHI)*KJ))}
Ejhn njhn 0 VALUE={EPH*0.5*(1+tanh((V(inn)-V(vcc)+VPRHI)*KJ))}
Ejlp njlp 0 VALUE={EPL*0.5*(1+tanh((V(vee)+VPRLO-V(inp))*KJ))}
Ejln njln 0 VALUE={EPL*0.5*(1+tanh((V(vee)+VPRLO-V(inn))*KJ))}
* (the two edge factors stay in one source: as separate nodes they made a
* transient with a 1 GH / 1 GF DC servo fail in tests/opamp_full_tests.cir)
Ewin nwin 0 VALUE={max(0.25*(1+tanh((V(nvc)+V(nsq)-V(vee)-ICMLO)/VK))*(1+tanh((V(vcc)-ICMHI-V(nvc)
+ +V(nsq))/VK)),1e-6) *(1-V(njhp))*(1-V(njhn))*(1-V(njlp))*(1-V(njln))}

* ---- phase reversal through an input junction (PRHI, PRLO) ---------------------
* In a bipolar or JFET input pair a junction of the input device becomes forward
* biased when an input goes far enough beyond the range: the base-collector
* junction of an npn (at the top), the gate-drain junction of a p-JFET (at the
* bottom). Its current flows from the input pin straight into the high-impedance
* node of the first stage, in the direction opposite to the normal signal, and
* the saturated device no longer works as an amplifier. J (0..1) is the
* conduction of each junction (nodes njhp, njhn, njlp, njln above, used by Bwin,
* Bprp, Bprn and Bpr): a logistic step of thermal width centred VPRHI
* below vcc (VPRLO above vee). At full conduction the input pin carries IPR
* (into the pin at the top, out of it at the bottom), the stage gm is switched
* off, and PRHI*IMAX (PRLO*IMAX) is injected into the gain node: the output runs
* to the wrong rail at about PRHI*SR, faster than the slew rate for PRHI > 1.
* The output flips at half conduction, where the input current is 0.9*IPR, so
* a source resistance RS prevents the reversal while 0.9*RS*IPR exceeds the
* overdrive. IPR is about the tail current of the input pair (0 = SR*30pF).
* pin current: the junction logistic shifted by DJ = VT*ln(9), i.e. 9J/(1+8J)
* (top junction: from the pin to vcc; bottom junction: from vee out of the pin)
Gprp inp vcc VALUE={IPRV*9*V(njhp)/(1+8*V(njhp))}
Gprn inn vcc VALUE={IPRV*9*V(njhn)/(1+8*V(njhn))}
Gplp vee inp VALUE={IPRV*9*V(njlp)/(1+8*V(njlp))}
Gpln vee inn VALUE={IPRV*9*V(njln)/(1+8*V(njln))}
* Injected current: IMAX*PR*J^(1+L), L = max(log2(PR),0). For PR >= 1 it
* equals the collapsing stage current IMAX*(1-J) at J = 1/2 whatever PR is, so
* the output flips at J = 1/2; at full conduction it is PR*IMAX.
.param LPH={max(log(max(PRHI,1e-3))/log(2),0)}
.param LPL={max(log(max(PRLO,1e-3))/log(2),0)}
.param AH={IMAXV*PRHI}
.param AL={IMAXV*PRLO}
Gpr nref n1 VALUE={AH*(pwr(V(njhn),(1+LPH))-pwr(V(njhp),(1+LPH)))+AL*(pwr(V(njlp),(1+LPL))
+ -pwr(V(njln),(1+LPL)))}

* ---- input-pair tail capacitance (KJUMP) ----------------------------------------
* The tail (common) node of the input pair follows the higher (KJUMP > 0,
* npn/nmos) or lower (KJUMP < 0, pnp/pmos) input (Solomon, IEEE JSSC 1974).
* TPL = sign(KJUMP). When a fast input step drives the tail, its charging
* current u = TPL*I(Vtl) > 0 is
* steered to the gain node, so the output of a follower jumps by |KJUMP| times
* the input step (in the uA741 the charge reaches the joined bases of the
* lateral pnps through an emitter-base junction, with current gain). On the
* other edge the tail follows the output slowly; its discharge current is
* small and is not modelled (u < 0 is ignored). Only input common-mode motion
* matters: a resistive inverter shows a smaller effect (its summing node moves
* while the output slews); small-signal analyses are unaffected.
.param TPL={IF(KJUMP<0, -1, 1)}
.param KTC={max(abs(KJUMP),1e-12)}
Etl ntl0 0 VALUE={V(nvc)+(0.5*TPL)*sqrt((V(inp)-V(inn))*(V(inp)-V(inn))+2.5e-3)}
* Ctl has a noiseless series resistance (time constant 1/(2*pi*FP3V)): the
* sensed current is then a smooth state, not the step-to-step ringing that
* trapezoidal integration gives in a capacitor driven by an ideal source
* (the max() below would rectify that ringing).
.param RTL={1/(PI2*FP3V*KTC*C1V)}
Vtl  ntl0 ntl 0
Gtl ntl ntlc VALUE={(1/RTL)*V(ntl,ntlc)}
Ctl  ntlc nref {KTC*C1V}
Rtllk ntlc nref 1e15

* ---- input stage + dominant pole (tanh gives slew limiting) ------------------
* The current limit IL = IMAXV + IDIFV*tanh(4*GM0*vd/IMAXV) moves smoothly from
* SRF*C1V (vd < 0) to SRR*C1V (vd > 0) well before the tanh saturates, so the
* output slews at SRR up and SRF down. The small-signal gm stays GM0 at vd = 0
* (IL cancels to first order); with SRR = SRF the stage is the plain tanh.
Ggm nref n1 VALUE={tanh(GM0*V(vd)/(IMAXV+IDIFV*tanh((4*GM0/IMAXV)*V(vd))))*((IMAXV+IDIFV*tanh((4
+ *GM0/IMAXV)*V(vd)))*V(nwin)+max(TPL*I(Vtl),0))}
G1 n1 nref VALUE={(1/R1V)*V(n1,nref)}
C1   n1 nref {C1V}
R1lk n1 nref 1e15
* soft clamp of the gain node to the swing limits (fast overdrive recovery)
Ehi nhi 0 VALUE={V(vcc)-VHD}
Elo nlo 0 VALUE={V(vee)+VLD}
Eex0 nex0 0 VALUE={V(n1)-V(nhi)}
Eex1 nex1 0 VALUE={V(n1)-V(nlo)}
Gclp n1 nref VALUE={(IMAXV/1e-3)*(max(V(nex0),0)+min(V(nex1),0))}

* ---- higher-order poles (noiseless 1k / C sections) --------------------------
G2   nref n2 n1 nref 1m
G2r  n2 nref n2 nref 1m
C2   n2 nref {1/(PI2*FP2V*1e3)}
R2lk n2 nref 1e15
G3   nref n3 n2 nref 1m
G3r  n3 nref n3 nref 1m
C3   n3 nref {1/(PI2*FP3V*1e3)}
R3lk n3 nref 1e15

* ---- output stage ----------------------------------------------------------------
* Norton output: I = (V(n3)-V(out) with crossover dead-zone)/ROUT, hard-limited
Evxmd nvxmd 0 VALUE={1e3*(V(n3)-V(out))}   ; vx in mV
Eicmd nicmd 0 VALUE={1e3*min(ISRC,max(-ISNK,((1e-3*V(nvxmd))-0.5*VXO*tanh((1e-3*V(nvxmd))/(VXO 
+ +1e-9)))/ROUT))}
* smooth split into source (from VCC) and sink (to VEE) currents:
* Bsrc - Bsnk = Icmd exactly, and the slope is well-defined at I = 0
* DC output resistance ROUTDC: the output current loads the gain node, which
* drops by Iout*ROUTDC at DC; C1 shunts it above the dominant pole
Godc n1 nref VALUE={(ROUTDC/R1V)*1e-3*V(nicmd)}
Gsrc vcc out VALUE={0.5e-3*(V(nicmd)+sqrt(V(nicmd)*V(nicmd)+1e-6))}
Gsnk out vee VALUE={0.5e-3*(sqrt(V(nicmd)*V(nicmd)+1e-6)-V(nicmd))}
Iq   vcc vee DC {ICCQ}
Cout out vcc {0.5*COUT}
Cout2 out vee {0.5*COUT}
.ends OPAMP_FULL

*==============================================================================
* Example parameter sets (use with  X1 inp inn vcc vee out OPAMP_FULL PARAMS: ...)
*
* Generic CMOS rail-to-rail, 5 V single supply, 5 MHz, 5 V/us:
*   A0=100k GBW=5Meg SR=5Meg FP2=15Meg FP3=50Meg VOS=2m IB=1p IOS=0.5p
*   RID=1T CID=2p RIC=2T CIC=1.5p CMRR=10k FCMRR=5k PSRR=10k
*   FPSRR=1k VCCN=5 VEEN=0 ENOISE=15n FCEN=1k INOISE=0.1f ICCQ=1m
*   VHD=0.05 VLD=0.05 ICMHI=-0.1 ICMLO=-0.1 VK=0.05 ROUT=100 ISRC=40m
*   ISNK=40m VXO=0 COUT=2p
*   (RIC=2T / RID=1T are fine; the Gmin of the simulator dominates.)
*   VCMN defaults to 2.5 V here (mid-supply). FCMRR = 5k leaves 20 dB of
*   CMRR at GBW; FP2 and FP3 could be left at 0 (3*GBW, 10*GBW).
*==============================================================================
